q(2,'b - False','A function may contain several return statements. Executing any one of them exits that invocation; there is no rule limiting the source code to one return statement.')
code('int absolute(int x) {\n    if (x < 0) return -x;\n    return x;\n}')
q(3,'b - False: compilation error','The + operators group left to right. The first operation is "hello" + ",", involving two string literals, not a std::string. C++ does not define concatenation by adding two pointers. The later std::string("world") does not repair that earlier invalid expression.')
code('std::string str2 = std::string("hello") + "," + "world";')
q(4,'b - False as written','A normal insertion overload returns std::ostream&amp; so that it returns the same stream and supports chaining. However, std::ostream has a deleted copy constructor. Returning an existing stream by value does not simply make a new copy: it fails to compile. The first part describes the right pattern; the last claim makes the full statement false. [1]')
code('std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& out, const Point& pt) {\n    out << pt.x << "," << pt.y;\n    return out;\n}')
q(5,'b - False','Effective size is the number of elements in use; allocated size is capacity. Effective size can equal capacity when the storage is full. For a valid representation: 0 &lt;= effective size &lt;= allocated size.')
q(6,'a - True','Each declarator needs its own *. Thus int* p1, p2 declares a pointer p1 and an int p2. Whitespace does not change the meaning: p3 is a pointer and p4 is an int.')
q(7,'a - True in the intended setting','For ordinary objects and built-in operators, &amp;x obtains x\'s address and * dereferences it, so *&amp;x denotes x. Similarly, &amp;*p recovers the pointer value. This introductory identity is not permission to read through invalid pointers; overloaded operators can also behave differently.')
q(9,'b - False','begin() denotes the first element; end() denotes the position one past the last element. end() is a stopping marker and must not be dereferenced.')
new();add('Short questions | Q11-Q22','h')
q(11,'c - 7','Multiplication, division and remainder have equal precedence and associate left to right. The left division is integer division; 1.0 only makes the right-hand parenthesized expression floating point.')
code('9 * (8-7) / 6       = 9 / 6 = 1\n5 * 4 % 3           = 20 % 3 = 2\n2 + 1.0             = 3.0\nTotal               = 1 + 2 * 3.0 = 7.0')
q(12,'c - private','Private data members support encapsulation: public member functions control how the object\'s state can be observed or changed and can preserve its invariants. friend is not an access level.')
q(14,'b - 64 GB','A 36-bit address gives 2^36 distinct byte addresses. Using the question\'s binary unit definition, 2^36 / 2^30 = 2^6 = 64 GB. This is the theoretical addressable capacity, not necessarily installed memory.')
q(15,'b - 0x2024 and 2021','pi stores the address of i; ppi stores the address of pi. Dereferencing ppi once gives pi; twice gives i\'s value.')
code('pi   = &i  = 0x2020\nppi  = &pi = 0x2024\n*ppi = pi  = 0x2020\n**ppi = i  = 2021')
q(20,'d - Constant call by reference','Use const T&amp;: this avoids copying the object and prevents modification through that parameter. A non-const reference allows changes; passing by value creates a separate parameter object.')
code('void inspect(const LargeObject& object);')
q(21,'b, c, d - with a wording caveat','Encapsulation, inheritance and polymorphism are the likely intended distinguishing OOP features. Abstraction is also fundamental to OOP, but it is shared with procedural and other programming styles. Because the question explicitly asks what distinguishes OOP, b, c, d is the best-fit selection. If the instructor uses the broader “four pillars” convention, a, b, c, d may be expected; the official marking is unverified.')
q(22,'a, c - Number and types of parameters','For the ordinary function overloading in this question, parameter names do not distinguish overloads, and changing only the return type is not enough. Different numbers or types of parameters can distinguish them.')
new();add('Memory and classes | Q24-Q30','h')
q(24,'a, c, d','<b>a: typename</b> is a reserved keyword.<br/><b>b: type_name</b> is legal: underscores are allowed.<br/><b>c: type-name</b> is not a single identifier; the hyphen is a minus token.<br/><b>d: 2typenames</b> is illegal because an identifier cannot begin with a digit.')
q(25,'a, b, c','The lecture memory model distinguishes static storage, heap storage for dynamic allocation, and stack storage for ordinary function activations/local variables. A queue is a data structure, not a separate memory region. These are the course\'s conventional implementation terms; the C++ language describes storage durations more abstractly.')
q(26,'a, c, d','<b>a:</b> Dynamic allocation reserves memory at runtime and returns an address that a pointer can hold.<br/><b>b:</b> False in ordinary allocation: new/malloc select the storage location. A pointer records that address; it does not generally choose an arbitrary heap address for the object.<br/><b>c:</b> A pointer compactly refers to a large structure without copying it.<br/><b>d:</b> Pointers can represent connections between nodes, as in a linked list.')
add('Reading caveat: “specify” in option b is imprecise. The answer above reads it as choosing the allocation address. Merely storing/referencing an address would be true, but is a different claim. Placement new constructs in existing storage; it does not make ordinary heap allocation choose any requested address.','c')
q(27,'c, d - &amp;p and (p+1)','Assume p is an ordinary raw pointer and the relevant operations are valid. p is an lvalue naming a pointer object. *p is an lvalue denoting the pointed-to object. &amp;p and p+1 produce pointer values (prvalues); neither denotes a pointer object you can assign into. Also, not every lvalue is modifiable: a const object is still an lvalue.')
code('p = anotherPointer; // potentially valid\n*p = 5;             // potentially valid for a writable int\n// &p = ...;        // invalid assignment target\n// (p+1) = ...;     // invalid assignment target')
